{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nDiscovered while widening the MCP model/schema drift gate for mirror F5\n(`62eb-5e6f-512a-420d`). That gate's registry covered 11 of 32 public `*Out` models. Deriving\nthe registry — every model whose `<snake_case>.schema.json` exists — brings 9 more models under\nthe check, and four of them are ALREADY DRIFTED.\n\n## The measured gap: 41 undeclared schema properties\n\n- **CreateResultOut** (create_result.schema.json) — 1: `title`\n- **GateResultOut** (gate_result.schema.json) — 5: `ac_items`, `criteria_count`, `file_impact`, `keyword_count`, `line_count`\n- **NextBatchOut** (next_batch.schema.json) — 12: `available_pool`, `batch`, `batch_size`, `epic_title`, `skipped_blocked`, `skipped_blocked_story`, `skipped_design_awaiting`, `skipped_in_progress`, `skipped_manual_awaiting`, `skipped_needs_planning`, `skipped_overlap`, `tasks`\n- **TicketStateOut** (ticket_state.schema.json) — 23: `attestations`, `author`, `bridge_alerts`, `bridge_project`, `close_class`, `close_reason`, `completion_expectation`, `created_at`, `creation_channel`, `creation_channel_inferred`, `detected_by`, `env_id`, `force_close_reason`, `last_reopened_at`, `preconditions_summary`, `repos`, `reverts`, `source_author`, `source_created_at`, `source_env`, `source_id`, `updated_at`, `verify_commands`\n\n## Why this is separate from F5\n\nF5 fixes the GATE — it makes the check enum-aware and derives its registry so nothing can be\nsilently omitted again. Closing these 41 gaps is a different change with a different risk\nprofile: `_mcp_models` supplies the MCP tools' published `outputSchema`, so declaring a field\nCHANGES THE WIRE CONTRACT clients read. That deserves its own review rather than riding along\ninside a gate refactor.\n\nF5 therefore baselines these four models as declared exceptions naming THIS ticket, so the gate\nis green at rest while any NEW omission fails. Standard ratchet shape, the same one\n`check_complexity_baseline.py` and `check_mechanism_delta.py` use.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] AC1: each of the four models declares the properties its canonical schema defines, or the\n      schema is corrected where the property is genuinely not part of that payload. Verified by\n      the drift gate reporting zero missing declarations for them with their baseline entries\n      REMOVED.\n- [x] AC2: the baseline exception entries added by F5 are deleted, not merely emptied, so the\n      gate's exception map returns to naming only genuine permissive omissions. Verified by\n      grepping the gate for this ticket id and finding none.\n- [x] AC3: any property deliberately NOT declared carries a stated reason in the gate's\n      permissive-omissions map. Verified by reading each remaining entry.\n- [x] AC4 [non-codebase]: the wire-contract impact is stated — for each newly declared field,\n      whether MCP clients could already observe it in practice. Verified by a comment on this\n      ticket recording the assessment.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `python -m rebar.schemas.check_mcp_models --check` — zero drift with the baseline removed.\n- `python -m pytest tests/interfaces/facades/test_mcp_model_schema_drift_heldout.py -q`.\n\n## Completion evidence (2026-09-03) — Gerrit change 2556 MERGED\n\n- **AC1** — 39 of the 41 properties are declared on `CreateResultOut`, `GateResultOut`, `NextBatchOut` and `TicketStateOut`; the remaining 2 are permissive omissions with stated reasons (AC3). `python -m rebar.schemas.check_mcp_models --check` reports \"MCP model schema drift gate OK — no missing declarations\" with the baseline removed. The property list was derived authoritatively by running `find_drift()` with `BASELINED_OMISSIONS` monkeypatched empty, and matched exactly.\n- **AC2** — `BASELINED_OMISSIONS` is DELETED, not emptied: the dict, its comment block, and its merge line in `find_drift` are all gone. `grep -c \"BASELINED_OMISSIONS\\|3a02\" src/rebar/schemas/check_mcp_models.py` returns **0**.\n- **AC3** — two deliberate omissions, each with a reason in the permissive-omissions map. `CreateResultOut.title`: only the CLI's `create --output json` emits it (`_commands/composer.py:481`); every MCP create tool returns `rebar.create_ticket(return_alias=True)`, which drops title — declaring it would advertise a permanently-null title on a ticket that always has one. `NextBatchOut.tasks`: belongs to the CLI-only `next-batch --limit=0` reduced variant (`_engine_support/next_batch.py:559`); the library/MCP path emits `batch` and never `tasks`.\n- **AC4** — wire-contract impact assessed and recorded in a comment on this ticket. **All 39 newly declared fields were ALREADY on the wire** as undeclared `extra=\"allow\"` pass-throughs, verified against this store's real tickets plus a union over 200 closed ones, so the change DOCUMENTS AN EXISTING SHAPE rather than altering what clients receive. That is a materially lower risk profile than F5 assumed when it deferred them.\n\nHazard surfaced and handled during implementation: declaring an optional pydantic field makes it EMIT, and 15 of the properties have canonical types admitting no null, so a naive declaration made real payloads violate their own schema (8 contract tests caught it). A new `_OmitUnsetOut` base pops never-set fields at serialization, so the declaration appears in `outputSchema` while the emitted bytes are unchanged. Timestamps are typed `int | str | None` rather than bare `int`, because `js_safe_result` would otherwise coerce them to a lossy number on FastMCP re-validation (bug 6fe7)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788420695401622001,"uuid":"1242d70a-fe9d-4e34-ab0e-2778f61cfaad"}